I. Introduction

oVirt aaa-jdbc extension is a part of oVirt since 3.6.0. By default 'internal'
profile (sometimes also called domain) is provided by aaa-jdbc extension in
oVirt 3.6 (during upgrade from oVirt 3.5 'internal' profile is converted from
legacy provider to the new aaa-jdbc provider).

It's also possible to configure additional profiles provided by aaa-jdbc, but
configuration and upgrade of these profiles have to be done manually (see
below).



II. Configuration of 'internal' aaa-jdbc profile

Configuration is completely handled by engine-setup, there are no manual steps
required.



III. Configuration of a new custom aaa-jdbc profile

To configure a new custom profile which uses aaa-jdbc extension please execute
following steps:

1. Setup database for aaa-jdbc extension

   Please replace DB_NAME, DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD with real values:

   su - postgres -c "psql -d template1 << __EOF__
    create user DB_USER password 'DB_PASSWORD';
    create database DB_NAME owner DB_USER template template0
        encoding 'UTF8' lc_collate 'en_US.UTF-8' lc_ctype 'en_US.UTF-8';
   __EOF__
   "

2. Configure PostgreSQL

   Please add following line into /var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf (please
   replace DB_NAME and DB_USER with real values):

     host    DB_NAME    DB_USER    0.0.0.0/0       md5
     host    DB_NAME    DB_USER    ::0/0           md5

   These line must be located prior to following lines:

     host    all        all        127.0.0.1/32    ident
     host    all        all        ::1/128         ident

   After that please restart postgresql service.

3. Populate database for aaa-jdbc extension

   Please replace DB_HOST, DB_NAME, DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD with
   real values:

   PGPASSWORD="DB_PASSWORD" \
       /usr/share/ovirt-engine-extension-aaa-jdbc/dbscripts/schema.sh \
       -s DB_HOST \
       -p DB_PORT \
       -d DB_NAME \
       -u DB_USER \
       -c apply

4. Setup AAA profile

   Select name of your profile (it will be visible to users during login) and
   replace PROFILE with selected value:

   # Copy example configuration files and rename according to PROFILE
   cp /usr/share/ovirt-engine-extension-aaa-jdbc/examples/extension.d/authn.properties \
       /etc/ovirt-engine/extensions.d/PROFILE-authn.properties
   cp /usr/share/ovirt-engine-extension-aaa-jdbc/examples/extension.d/authz.properties \
       /etc/ovirt-engine/extensions.d/PROFILE-authz.properties

   # Create /etc/ovirt-engine/aaa directory if necessary
   cp /usr/share/ovirt-engine-extension-aaa-jdbc/examples/aaa/profile.properties \
       /etc/ovirt-engine/aaa/PROFILE.properties

   Edit created PROFILE*.properties files and replace variables surrounded by
   @ with real values.

5. Restart ovirt-engine service and check /var/log/ovirt-engine/engine.log
   to see if extension is initialized successfully according to PROFILE.

6. Setup users and groups

   Setup your users and groups using ovirt-aaa-jdbc-tool and specify database
   configuration using --db-config command line option:

     ovirt-aaa-jdbc-tool \
         --db-config=/etc/ovirt-engine/aaa/PROFILE.properties \
         OPTIONS

7. Login to webadmin using existing administrator account and assign desired
   permissions to users/groups defined in your PROFILE


Password policy
---------------

The default policy requires a password of at least twelve characters containing
an uppercase letter, a lowercase letter, a number, and a special character.
It also rejects a password identical to the user name, repeated patterns, and
four-character alphabetic, numeric, or adjacent-keyboard sequences (for
example, `1234`, `qwer`, and `asdf`).

The optional checks can be selected by updating the corresponding row in the
profile database's `settings` table:

* `PASSWORD_REQUIRE_SPECIAL`
* `PASSWORD_REJECT_REPEATED`
* `PASSWORD_REJECT_KEYBOARD_SEQUENCES`
* `PASSWORD_HISTORY_LIMIT`
* `PASSWORD_HISTORY_DAYS`

Set a Boolean option to `FALSE` or a numeric option to `0` to disable it.
`PASSWORD_HISTORY_DAYS` is configurable from 0 through 90 days. Changes take
effect at the next configured settings refresh or after restarting the engine.

`user add` creates a disabled-password account; assign its initial password
with `user password-reset`. Unless the explicit administrative `--force` or
pre-encrypted import path is used, that command applies the same policy as a
user-initiated password change. The default policy maps to the type (2)
requirements as follows:

* `MIN_LENGTH=12` exceeds the mandatory six-character minimum.
* `PASSWORD_COMPLEXITY` requires at least one uppercase letter, lowercase
  letter, and digit. Set an individual group's `min` to `-1` to disable it.
* `PASSWORD_REQUIRE_SPECIAL` controls the optional special-character check.
* Equality with the user ID is always rejected, case-insensitively.
* `PASSWORD_REJECT_REPEATED` rejects three or more adjacent occurrences of the
  same letter or digit.
* `PASSWORD_REJECT_KEYBOARD_SEQUENCES` rejects four or more horizontal keyboard
  characters, including `qwer`, `asdf`, and `1234`, in either direction and
  without case sensitivity. It also rejects four alphabetic or numeric code
  points in ascending or descending order, such as `abcd` and `4321`.
* `PASSWORD_HISTORY_LIMIT` protects the configured number of previous
  passwords, while `PASSWORD_HISTORY_DAYS` protects every password used in the
  configured period. Set both to `0` to disable previous-password reuse checks;
  the day setting cannot exceed 90.


Account unlock policy
---------------------

`user unlock` starts the account with a clean authentication-failure state: it
sets the unlock time, resets `consecutive_failures`, and deletes that user's
rows from `failed_logins` in the same database transaction. Setting a future
unlock time while automatically locking an account does not delete interval
history, so an expired lock cannot be used to evade `MAX_FAILURES_PER_INTERVAL`.

Do not disable or raise `MAX_FAILURES_PER_INTERVAL` merely to recover a locked
account. Use `user unlock` instead. The secure defaults retain 20 failures per
24-hour interval and a 60-minute lock; weakening the interval limit can permit
substantially more password guesses.


Vault Transit protected passphrases
-----------------------------------

Encrypted database configuration files and the encryptor passphrase selected
by `secret_file` may use the authenticated `OVVLT001` envelope. Its 256-bit
data-encryption key is wrapped by a
non-exportable Vault Transit key, while AES-256-GCM protects the passphrase and
authenticates the envelope metadata. Configure `vault_transit` in
`/etc/ovirt-engine/encryptor/config.json` with `enabled`, `address`, `mount`,
`key_name`, `token_file`, `ca_cert`, and `timeout`. The token and configuration
must not be group/other writable, and the token needs only `update` on the
configured `transit/decrypt/<key_name>` endpoint.

HTTPS is required. Plain HTTP is accepted only for a loopback address when
`allow_plaintext_loopback` is explicitly enabled for development. Reading an
`OVVLT001` passphrase fails closed when Vault is unavailable, sealed, rejects
the token, or returns an invalid key, and when any envelope metadata or
ciphertext authentication fails.

`ovirt-aaa-jdbc-tool` skips `engine-prolog.sh` when it detects an encrypted
database entry in `engine.conf.d`. Such files are binary envelopes rather than
shell fragments; sourcing one would fail before the Java decryptor can run. In
that case the launcher constructs the standard oVirt module path directly and
accepts `JAVA_HOME`, `JBOSS_HOME`, `ENGINE_USR`, `ENGINE_ETC`, and
`ENGINE_JAVA_MODULEPATH` overrides. Plaintext installations retain the existing
`engine-prolog.sh` behavior.

The packaged JDBC module loads PostgreSQL JDBC together with its
`ongres-scram` and `ongres-stringprep` dependencies. This is required when the
PostgreSQL role uses SCRAM-SHA-256 authentication; omitting those dependencies
causes the driver to fail before it can authenticate.



IV. Upgrading 'internal' profile

aaa-jdbc package and 'internal' profile is automatically upgraded during
engine-setup execution, no manual action is required.

ATTENTION: If your setup contains also custom aaa-jdbc profiles (more info
in chapter III. Configuration of a new custom aaa-jdbc profile), please
continue with steps described in chapter V. Upgrading custom profiles.



V. Upgrading custom profiles

To upgrade custom aaa-jdbc profiles please do following steps:

1. Stop oVirt engine service

     EL6:          service ovirt-engine stop
     EL7/Fedora:   systemctl stop ovirt-engine

2. For each existing custom aaa-jdbc profile please execute following command:

     PGPASSWORD="DB_PASSWORD" \
       /usr/share/ovirt-engine-extension-aaa-jdbc/dbscripts/schema.sh \
       -s DB_HOST \
       -p DB_PORT \
       -d DB_NAME \
       -u DB_USER \
       -e DB_SCHEMA \
       -c apply

   Please replace DB_HOST, DB_NAME, DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD and DB_SCHEMA (if
   profile was configured with different than 'public' schema) with values
   that are specified in database configuration for each profile (usually
   located in /etc/ovirt-engine/aaa/PROFILE_NAME.properties).

3. Start oVirt engine service

     EL6:          service ovirt-engine start
     EL7/Fedora:   systemctl start ovirt-engine
